\ ff-test: lines
\ Words that take a name or a string from the input - `:`, `create`, `'`,
\ `forget`, `is`, `postpone`, `."`, ... - read it themselves when they run,
\ from the same line, as standard Forth words parse the input stream.
\
\ They used to set a flag for the interpreter to act on at the next token,
\ wherever it came from: a word that ran `create` twice could only name
\ the second word, and a name could turn up lines later.

: p 32 emit . ;

\ A word that parses, run inside another, reads what follows the call.
: two-creates create create ;
two-creates first second
' first ' second <> p cr
: tick ' ;
tick dup ' dup = p cr

\ The name or string has to follow on the same line.
:
create
'
: late ."
3 p cr

\ And be the right kind of token.
' 42
: 42
.( 42
clear

\ `is` and `postpone` read theirs the same way.
defer hook
: ten 10 ;
' ten is hook hook p cr
: my-then postpone then ; immediate
: absval dup 0< if negate my-then ;
-7 absval p cr

\ `forget` can run inside a word, but not remove one that is running:
\ its code would be freed under it.
: forgetter "forget victim" evaluate ;
: victim 1 ;
forgetter p cr
victim
: self-forget "forget self-forget" evaluate ;
self-forget p cr

\ A `{` signature may span lines, but not outlive the input an error
\ discards: `--` here is a word again.
: sig-part "{ a 5" evaluate p ; immediate
: sig-rest sig-part -- b } ;
